    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d441-a1">

                <pb facs="0770" n="700" xml:id="pg700"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lincoln to Mr.
                            Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >War Department</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Washington
                                City</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1883-10-19"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 19,
                    1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department duly received your letter of
                    the 3d ultimo, inclosing, with request for an expression of the views of this
                    Department thereon, a copy of a note from the Mexican chargé d’affaires <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ad interim</hi> here of August 16 last, relative to the
                    return of the articles stolen from citizens of Mexico by the Indians recently
                    captured by General Crook in the Sierra Madre, and suggesting that a formal
                    agreement be entered into between the United States and Mexico providing for the
                    return in future of such articles as may be recovered from captured Indians when
                    ascertained to belong to the citizens of either Government.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to state that the subject was duly referred to General
                    Crook, who is thoroughly conversant therewith, and from whose report thereon,
                    dated the 30fch ultimo, a copy of which is herewith inclosed, it would seem to
                    be difficult, if not wholly impracticable, to carry out the suggestion presented
                    by the chargé d’affaires of Mexico in his above-mentioned note.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed>ROBERT T. LINCOLN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
